/*!
    \example robotarmcollision
    \title Robot Arm Collision
    \ingroup qtros2-examples
    \meta sortkey {60}
    \brief Importing a URDF robot as a Qt Quick 3D module with physics collision
    shapes, driven either by an animation or by live ROS 2 joint states.

    \c{Robot Arm Collision} turns a URDF robot description into a Qt Quick 3D
    scene at build time and adds \l{Qt Quick 3D Physics} collision detection. The
    arm sweeps through a static box obstacle; while any link overlaps the box it
    tints red and an on-screen "Collision detected" banner flashes. The arm can
    be driven two ways: by a built-in animation, or live from
    \c sensor_msgs/JointState messages arriving over ROS 2.

    \image robotarmcollision/robotarmcollision.png "The Robot Arm Collision example: while the arm sweeps into the obstacle, the box turns red and a Collision detected banner appears."

    \section1 Running the Example

    \list 1
        \li Source your ROS 2 environment and launch Qt Creator from that shell.
        \li Open \c examples/robotarmcollision/CMakeLists.txt in Qt Creator, then
            build and run.
        \li With \b Animate checked, the arm runs the demo animation and
            periodically collides with the obstacle.
        \li With \b Animate unchecked, the arm follows incoming
            \c sensor_msgs/JointState messages on \c /joint_states. Publish joint
            states to move it — for example with a joint-state publisher loading
            \c simple_arm.urdf, in a separate ROS-sourced terminal:
            \badcode
            ros2 launch examples/robotarmcollision/simple_arm.launch.py
            \endcode
    \endlist

    \section1 Importing a URDF at Build Time

    Unlike the other examples, this one does not import a ROS \e message package —
    it imports a \e robot. \c qt_ros2_import_urdf() converts \c simple_arm.urdf
    into a Qt Quick 3D QML module named \c SimpleArm and links it into the target.
    The \c PHYSICS option generates \l{Qt Quick 3D Physics} rigid bodies and
    collision shapes from the URDF collision meshes, and \c ROS_BRIDGE generates a
    companion type that feeds live joint states into the model:

    \badcode
    qt_ros2_import_urdf(robotarmcollisionapp
        simple_arm.urdf
        PHYSICS
        ROS_BRIDGE
    )
    \endcode

    The \c qt_ros2_import_urdf() function also accepts options for the QML module
    URI, scene scale, and output directory, and it re-runs the importer
    automatically whenever the URDF file changes — so a plain \c{cmake --build}
    picks up edits to the robot description.

    \section1 Assembling the Scene

    The generated module provides three cooperating types:

    \list
        \li \c SimpleArmControl — the control object. It holds the joint angles
            (for example \c shoulderLiftAngle) and joint metadata
            (\c{jointInfos[n].upper} / \c lower limits), and — because
            \c PHYSICS was used — can emit collision overlap reports when
            \c sendTriggerReports is enabled.
        \li \c SimpleArm — the 3D model. It renders the links and is bound to a
            control object via its \c control property.
        \li \c RosBridge — the generated ROS bridge. Bound to the same control
            object, it applies incoming \c /joint_states to the arm.
    \endlist

    \qml
    import QtQuick3D
    import QtQuick3D.Physics
    import SimpleArm

    SimpleArmControl {
        id: armControl
        sendTriggerReports: true   // let links report overlaps to the obstacle
    }

    RosBridge {
        control: armControl
        // Drop joint messages while animating; otherwise drive from /joint_states.
        processMessages: !window.animate
    }

    View3D {
        SimpleArm { control: armControl }   // the 3D model, sharing the control
    }
    \endqml

    Toggling the \b Animate check box flips \c{RosBridge.processMessages}: when the
    built-in animation is running the bridge ignores ROS messages so the two drive
    sources never fight, and when it is off the arm follows \c /joint_states.

    \section1 Detecting Collisions

    The obstacle is a \c TriggerBody, which does not physically block the
    kinematic arm — it only reports overlaps. An \c overlaps counter is
    incremented and decremented as links enter and leave, which both tints the box
    red and flashes the banner:

    \qml
    TriggerBody {
        id: obstacle
        property int overlaps: 0
        collisionShapes: BoxShape { extents: Qt.vector3d(18, 18, 18) }

        onBodyEntered: { obstacle.overlaps++; banner.flash() }
        onBodyExited:  obstacle.overlaps = Math.max(0, obstacle.overlaps - 1)

        Model {
            source: "#Cube"
            materials: PrincipledMaterial {
                baseColor: obstacle.overlaps > 0 ? "red" : "#cccccc"
            }
        }
    }
    \endqml

    \sa {R6 Robot Teach Pendant}
*/
